Three things.

The roster is complete: Stratus is Khodapanah, Sabzi and Bakhtiari Moghaddam,
which was the one column of the sheet I could not read.

A third tab beside the two, for the shape of a week. Five days, each named for
what actually happens in it rather than for its slot — Saturday is the previous
mission delivered and the next one opened, Wednesday is Saturday rehearsed in
front of your own team. Each day says what it is, what happens there, and what
to bring; the current day marks itself, and Thursday and Friday get a line
saying there is nothing scheduled and that is deliberate.

Backups run two ways, because one machine holding the only copy is not a backup.
The server takes a dated copy of the data file at boot and once a day, keeping
thirty. That survives a container rebuild but not a lost disk, so the lead's
panel also hands out the whole thing as spreadsheets: four CSVs and the raw
JSON. Two details make those files actually open: a byte-order mark, without
which Excel renders Persian as mojibake, and an apostrophe in front of any text
starting with = or +, without which a spreadsheet reads a mentor's note as a
formula. Exports are lead-only — a mentor asking for one gets a 403, which is
the same rule as everywhere else in the panel.

